# Which changes need a person, and which do not.
#
# This file used to name `@milkmenot` for every path in the repository. That
# account does not exist, so it assigned nobody, required nothing and reviewed
# less — and because it covered `*`, turning the rule on would have blocked
# every pull request and undone auto-merge entirely.
#
# So it is deliberately short now. Almost everything here should merge itself
# the moment the checks go green: tests, components, copy, styling. The checks
# are a better reviewer for those than a tired person at eleven at night, and
# anything they get wrong is a redeploy away from being fixed.
#
# What is listed below is what a redeploy does not fix.
#
# On a repository with one maintainer this is not peer review — nobody else can
# approve, and GitHub does not let you approve your own pull request. What it
# actually buys is the end of auto-merge on these paths: a change here stops and
# waits to be merged on purpose, rather than landing at three in the morning
# because the tests happened to pass.
#
# GitHub's own "require review from code owners" does not do that here. It is
# inert unless a minimum approval count is set, and setting one would demand an
# approval on every pull request in the repository. `owned-paths.yml` reads the
# list below and asks for the `reviewed` label instead, which is a deliberate
# act one person can actually perform. This file stays the single list.
#
# See "Three paths do not auto-merge" in CLAUDE.md for the whole story.

# Migrations, policies and edge functions. A bad migration runs once against
# real data and cannot be taken back, and RLS is the only thing standing
# between the tables and the internet.
/supabase/                  @preijm

# The safety net itself: the checks, the alerting, the auto-merge, and this
# file. A change here can quietly switch off everything else that would have
# caught a change here.
/.github/                   @preijm

# Content-Security-Policy, Permissions-Policy, frame-ancestors. Loosened by
# accident, nothing fails and nothing tells you.
/public/_headers            @preijm
